    <meta name="dc.description" content="Growing anthropogenic pressure on aquatic environments has intensified processes of water quality degradation; compromising ecosystems, the health and wellness of populations that depend on natural resources, and associated economic activities. This study aims to assess the water quality of Lagoa da Concei&#231;&#227;o, in Florian&#243;polis, SC, Brazil, through two distinct and complementary approaches; the evaluation of potentially toxic chemical elements in nanoparticles (NPs) and ultrafine particles present in aquatic sediments collected between 2024 and 2025, and the analysis of the variables chlorophyll-a (CHL), total suspended matter (TSM), and absorption of dissolved organic matter at 443&amp;nbsp;nm (ADG443), obtained from Sentinel-3A/B OLCI satellite imagery between 2019 and 2025. Methodologically, aquatic sediments were collected at 18 sites across Lagoa da Concei&#231;&#227;o for the analysis of NPs and ultrafine particles. In parallel, the CHL, TSM, and ADG443 variables were retrieved from Sentinel-3A/B satellite imagery at 153 points processed in the Sentinel Application Platform (SNAP), with the resulting data subjected to K-means clustering and robust ANOVA to identify geospatial patterns. As a result, sediment analyses identified iron (Fe) nanoparticles containing up to 60% carbon, frequently associated with chromium (Cr), nickel (Ni), vanadium (V), zinc (Zn), and zirconium (Zr), in addition to the presence of plastic nanoparticles. Concurrently, the bio-optical variables of the water column exhibited elevated values of CHL (7.187&amp;nbsp;mg/m3), TSM (17.213&amp;nbsp;g/m3), and ADG443 (5.555&amp;nbsp;m&#8315;1) over the 2019&#8211;2025 period. The geospatial patterns obtained through K-means clustering revealed moderate-to-high concentrations of these indicators within the interior of Lagoa da Concei&#231;&#227;o and significantly lower values in the adjacent coastal areas. Taken together, these two distinct and complementary approaches provided evidence of water quality degradation in the study area."/>
