    <meta property="og:url" content="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10661-026-15991-x"/>
    <meta property="og:type" content="article"/>
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="SpringerLink"/>
    <meta property="og:title" content="Water quality assessment using sediment analysis and Sentinel-3-derived bio-optical variables in Lagoa da Conceição, Brazil - Environmental Monitoring and Assessment"/>
    <meta property="og:description" content="Growing anthropogenic pressure on aquatic environments has intensified processes of water quality degradation; compromising ecosystems, the health and wellness of populations that depend on natural resources, and associated economic activities. This study aims to assess the water quality of Lagoa da Concei&#231;&#227;o, in Florian&#243;polis, SC, Brazil, through two distinct and complementary approaches; the evaluation of potentially toxic chemical elements in nanoparticles (NPs) and ultrafine particles present in aquatic sediments collected between 2024 and 2025, and the analysis of the variables chlorophyll-a (CHL), total suspended matter (TSM), and absorption of dissolved organic matter at 443 nm (ADG443), obtained from Sentinel-3A/B OLCI satellite imagery between 2019 and 2025. Methodologically, aquatic sediments were collected at 18 sites across Lagoa da Concei&#231;&#227;o for the analysis of NPs and ultrafine particles. In parallel, the CHL, TSM, and ADG443 variables were retrieved from Sentinel-3A/B satellite imagery at 153 points processed in the Sentinel Application Platform (SNAP), with the resulting data subjected to K-means clustering and robust ANOVA to identify geospatial patterns. As a result, sediment analyses identified iron (Fe) nanoparticles containing up to 60% carbon, frequently associated with chromium (Cr), nickel (Ni), vanadium (V), zinc (Zn), and zirconium (Zr), in addition to the presence of plastic nanoparticles. Concurrently, the bio-optical variables of the water column exhibited elevated values of CHL (7.187 mg/m3), TSM (17.213 g/m3), and ADG443 (5.555 m&#8315;1) over the 2019&#8211;2025 period. The geospatial patterns obtained through K-means clustering revealed moderate-to-high concentrations of these indicators within the interior of Lagoa da Concei&#231;&#227;o and significantly lower values in the adjacent coastal areas. Taken together, these two distinct and complementary approaches provided evidence of water quality degradation in the study area."/>
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://media.springernature.com/m685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1007%2Fs10661-026-15991-x/MediaObjects/10661_2026_15991_Fig1_HTML.png"/>
    

        

